4: </style></head><body><main class="wrap"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">Learnimal / Proof School / UI exploration</div><h1 style="margin:8px 0;font-size:34px;letter-spacing:-.065em">A notes app that quietly becomes a mastery engine.</h1><p class="intro">The user enters through the familiar door: capture anything. The product then reveals a harder, denser path—evidence, explanation, retrieval, application, and verifiable output. This mockup uses the repository’s card roles, research/extraction, explicit AI preflights, pipelines, custom assistants, study scheduling, mission model, receipts, and undo as a single “proof of learning” loop. [file:1]</p><nav class="tabs" id="tabs"></nav><section id="design" class="page"><div class="conversation"><div class="panel"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">Fictional Nintendo design review</div><h2>“Do not make a school inside a phone. Make a place where people discover they can do hard things.”</h2><div class="turn"><b>Creative Director</b><p>“A novice opens a notes app because notes are safe. Let them capture a thought, link, page, or question. Do not demand they understand the system first.”</p></div><div class="turn alt"><b>Learning Architect</b><p>“But we cannot mistake activity for learning. A learner earns progress only when they can retrieve, explain, apply, test, or produce something under clear conditions.”</p></div><div class="turn blue"><b>Systems Lead</b><p>“The app already has the raw machinery: cards, chunks, recall, cloze, scheduling, research, extraction, agent profiles, commands, workspaces, provenance, and operation receipts. The design task is to make those feel like one instrument.”</p></div><div class="turn"><b>Creative Director</b><p>“Then the center is not a syllabus. It is a Proof Loop: claim a goal, gather material, make a prediction, test it, explain it, and leave an artifact that can be inspected.”</p></div><div class="turn alt"><b>Decision</b><p>“The UI begins as Capture. Each captured item receives one next move. Over time, the workspace becomes a living field of proof rather than a library of notes.”</p></div></div><div class="panel"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">Verified learning, expressed in UI</div><div class="principle"><b>Evidence is not mastery</b><p>Sources are retained, inspected, extracted, and cited. They are inputs, not a completion badge.</p></div><div class="principle"><b>Explanation is checked by use</b><p>The learner predicts before feedback, then applies the idea in a new scenario, experiment, or deliverable.</p></div><div class="principle"><b>AI makes proposals, not verdicts</b><p>It surfaces prerequisites, challenges scope, drafts questions, and suggests experiments. Its scope, web use, outputs, and source grounding are visible before execution.</p></div><div class="principle"><b>Mastery is a trail of artifacts</b><p>Notes, claims, experiments, source provenance, recall performance, and actual deliverables can be inspected later.</p></div><div class="principle"><b>Hardness comes from compression</b><p>Instead of more lectures, each loop forces a useful act: decide, retrieve, teach, test, or build.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="capture" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Learnimal</div><div class="sub">Start anywhere. We will find the next useful move.</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">CAPTURE</div></header><div class="body"><div class="hero"><div class="mono label">Your first minute</div><h1>Put one thing you want to understand or make here.</h1><p>A rough thought, URL, question, copied passage, or project idea is enough. No course setup required.</p><textarea class="input" placeholder="I want to understand how real-time renderers keep a large world responsive on mobile…"></textarea><div class="hint">Captured items remain yours. The app suggests next steps; it does not take over.</div><button class="full">Save to my workspace</button><button class="link">Paste a source instead</button></div></div></div></section><section id="next" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Learnimal</div><div class="sub">Captured to: Untitled workspace</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--green)">SAVED</div></header><div class="body"><div class="seal"><i></i> One note saved. Nothing else changed.</div><div class="card"><div class="tag">Your note</div><h3>Mobile real-time renderer</h3><p>“I want to understand how real-time renderers keep a large world responsive on mobile…”</p></div><div class="section mono">Choose one useful next move</div><div class="choice"><button class="active">Explain this to me<br><small style="color:var(--muted)">Agent reads this note only and creates grounded concept cards.</small></button><button>Find the prerequisites<br><small style="color:var(--muted)">Identify likely missing concepts and questions.</small></button><button>Turn this into a learning mission<br><small style="color:var(--muted)">Define a proof, constraint, and first build.</small></button></div><nav class="dock"><button>CAPTURE</button><button>LIBRARY</button><button>REVIEW</button><button class="main">TOOLS</button></nav></div></div></section><section id="loop" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Proof loop</div><div class="sub">Goal: ship a mobile renderer prototype</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">01/05</div></header><div class="body"><section class="mission"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">Current proof</div><h2>Explain the bottleneck before choosing a fix.</h2><p>You are not “reading about rendering.” You are preparing to make a defensible design choice.</p><div class="trio"><div class="metric"><b>2</b><span>Evidence</span></div><div class="metric"><b>1</b><span>Unknown</span></div><div class="metric"><b>1</b><span>Test</span></div></div></section><div class="section mono">Predict first</div><div class="card amber"><div class="tag" style="color:var(--amber)">Question / answer before reveal</div><h3>What resource usually limits a mobile renderer first: CPU time, GPU time, memory, or network?</h3><p>Choose a hypothesis and state what measurement would change your mind.</p></div><div class="choice"><button>CPU time — I predict draw-call overhead</button><button>GPU time — I predict fill rate</button><button>It depends — I need a baseline</button></div><button class="full">Commit prediction</button><nav class="dock"><button>NOTE</button><button>HINT</button><button>SKIP</button><button class="main">ANSWER</button></nav></div></div></section><section id="evidence" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Evidence bench</div><div class="sub">Research is inspectable, not magic.</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--blue)">WEB</div></header><div class="body"><div class="seal"><i></i> Search completed through SearchGateway. Model did not browse.</div><div class="card blue"><div class="tag" style="color:var(--blue)">Official documentation / retained</div><h3>WebGPU concepts and limits</h3><p>Source candidate retained. Open, extract, or reject before using it as evidence.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="tag">Comparable project / caution</div><h3>Open-source mobile voxel renderer</h3><p>Useful implementation example; benchmark claims require independent verification.</p></div><div class="card rose"><div class="tag" style="color:var(--rose)">Gap</div><h3>No target-device benchmark yet</h3><p>Evidence cannot answer your performance question without a defined test target.</p></div><button class="full">Extract 1 retained source</button><button class="link">Create a cited brief after extraction</button><nav class="dock"><button>KEEP</button><button>OPEN</button><button>REJECT</button><button class="main">EXTRACT</button></nav></div></div></section><section id="build" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Build the proof</div><div class="sub">Experiment compiled from your uncertainty</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--amber)">TEST</div></header><div class="body"><div class="card amber"><div class="tag" style="color:var(--amber)">Experiment / draft</div><h3>Measure baseline frame time on one target device</h3><p><b>Hypothesis:</b> draw-call overhead is the bottleneck before fill rate.<br><b>Method:</b> render a fixed scene; collect CPU/GPU frame timing.<br><b>Success condition:</b> determine the limiting budget and next optimization.</p></div><div class="section mono">Before you run it</div><div class="choice"><button class="active">Target: Android mid-range device, 60 fps budget</button><button>Add materials or measurement</button><button>Ask agent to challenge this design</button></div><div class="receipt"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--green)">Why this counts</div><h3>A result can falsify your current belief.</h3><p>The output is not a “completed lesson.” It is a recorded decision with evidence, method, and measurement.</p></div><button class="full" style="margin-top:14px">Create experiment card</button><nav class="dock"><button>EDIT</button><button>PREREQS</button><button>CAPTURE</button><button class="main">CREATE</button></nav></div></div></section><section id="receipt" class="page"><div class="device"><header class="top"><div><div class="brand"><i></i>Learning record</div><div class="sub">Proof trail / inspectable progress</div></div><div class="mono" style="color:var(--green)">VERIFIED</div></header><div class="body"><section class="mission"><div class="mono" style="color:var(--lime)">Capability in progress</div><h2>Mobile rendering: first defensible claim</h2><p>You can now state a tested hypothesis, name the evidence used, and explain what you will do next.</p><div class="trio"><div class="metric"><b>04</b><span>Retrieved</span></div><div class="metric"><b>02</b><span>Applied</span></div><div class="metric"><b>01</b><span>Built</span></div></div></section><div class="section mono">Proof trail</div><div class="card"><div class="tag">Claim / learner-authored</div><h3>Draw-call overhead limits the baseline scene</h3><p>Supported by experiment result; marked provisional until repeated.</p></div><div class="card blue"><div class="tag" style="color:var(--blue)">Evidence / provenance available</div><h3>WebGPU concepts and limits</h3><p>Retained source → extracted material → used in experiment planning.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="tag">Practice / due tomorrow</div><h3>Recall: distinguish CPU, GPU, and memory bottlenecks</h3><p>Scheduled only after explicit enrollment.</p></div><button class="full">Generate a status gap report</button><nav class="dock"><button>REVIEW</button><button>STATUS</button><button>EXPORT</button><button class="main">TOOLS</button></nav></div></div></section></main><script>const p=[['design','Design review'],['capture','Start like notes'],['next','One next move'],['loop','Proof loop'],['evidence','Evidence bench'],['build','Build proof'],['receipt','Learning record']];const host=document.getElementById('tabs');p.forEach(([id,label],i)=>{const b=document.createElement('button');b.textContent=label;b.onclick=()=>{document.querySelectorAll('.page').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('show'));document.querySelectorAll('.tabs button').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('active'));document.getElementById(id).classList.add('show');b.classList.add('active')};host.append(b);if(i===0)b.click()})</script></body></html>
