  13: Do not begin broad implementation before writing:
  14: - docs/ui-system-implementation-plan.md
  15: 
  16: The plan must map every visual feature and user interaction below to:
  17: 1. Existing entities/use cases/workflows/controllers/presenters.
  18: 2. New modules required.
  19: 3. Persistence requirements.
  20: 4. Migration and backwards compatibility behavior.
  21: 5. Test coverage.
  22: 6. Exact files likely to change.
  23: 
  24: Do not remove existing working capabilities to simplify implementation.
  25: 
  26: ==================================================
  27: PRODUCT DEFINITION
  28: ==================================================
  29: 
  30: GRIOT is not a chatbot, productivity dashboard, online course platform, or generic notes app.
  31: 
  32: GRIOT is a learning shell.
  33: 
  34: A user may begin with:
  35: - A note.
  36: - A question.
  37: - A pasted excerpt.
  38: - A URL.
  39: - A source document.
  40: - A project idea.
  41: - A goal they cannot yet articulate clearly.
  42: 
  43: The system helps them progressively turn material into:
  44: - Questions.
  45: - Concepts.
  46: - Evidence.
  47: - Claims.
  48: - Prerequisites.
  49: - Experiments.
  50: - Tasks.
  51: - Deliverables.
  52: - Recall practice.
  53: - A visible proof trail.
  54: 
  55: The user should not need to understand commands, agent scopes, research provenance, FSRS, pipelines, or semantic roles on day one.
  56: 
  57: Those capabilities must remain fully available and discoverable as the user gains confidence.
  58: 
  59: The UI must use progressive disclosure:
  60: - Beginner surface: capture, understand, practice, make progress.
  61: - Intermediate surface: sources, prerequisites, experiments, status gaps, study generation.
  62: - Advanced surface: card selection, explicit pipelines, custom commands, custom assistants, prompt editing, assistant profiles, receipts, provenance, typed command expressions.
  63: 
  64: The app’s deepest promise is trust:
  65: - The user can always inspect where an output came from.
  66: - The app distinguishes user-provided material, deterministic app findings, agent hypotheses, and real web evidence.
  67: - AI never falsely claims it searched, read, extracted, cited, or completed an action.
  68: - Search is executed only by SearchGateway.
  69: - Extraction is executed only by ExtractionGateway.
  70: - Model use is always visible before and after execution.
  71: - No network/model action occurs without an explicit, readable preflight.
  72: - Errors are specific and actionable.
  73: - A failed model or web operation is never replaced by fake success.
  74: 
  75: ==================================================
  76: FOUNDATIONAL INTERACTION MODEL
  77: ==================================================
  78: 
  79: Preserve and make visible the Unix model:
  80: 
  81: - Cards are universal material.
  82: - Selected cards are stdin.
  83: - Commands are transformations.
  84: - Result cards are stdout.
  85: - Groups are folders/scopes.
  86: - Pipelines compose transformations.
  87: - Operation receipts are history.
  88: - Provenance is inspectable lineage.
  89: - Agent profiles are named, editable tools—not hidden personalities.
  90: - Web research is an external operation, never implied by generic “Ask.”
  91: - The canvas is the primary home surface.
  92: 
  93: However, do not make the user type terminal commands by default.
  94: 
  95: Mobile adaptation:
  96: - Touch-first action tiles are precomposed commands.
  97: - A touch action always maps to a real command or request object.
  98: - The user can reveal the underlying command/pipeline expression.
  99: - Typing is a power-user path, never required for normal use.
 100: - Selection creates an explicit visible input bundle.
 101: - The visible actions change based on selected card roles and state.
 102: - Every action has large, stable touch targets.
 103: - The bottom thumb zone is reserved for the highest-frequency actions.
 104: - Avoid menus inside menus and icon-only critical controls.
 105: 
 106: Core product rule:
 107: “Touch is the friendly syntax. Pipelines are the underlying truth.”
