<file path="docs/architecture.md">
  1: # Architecture
  2: 
  3: GRIOT is built as four layers with a single rule: **dependencies only ever point
  4: inward.** `src/__tests__/dependencyRule.test.ts` enforces it on every run, so the rule is
  5: part of the build rather than a convention people remember.
  6: 
  7: ```
  8: entities/    Pure domain. Cards, workspaces, missions, schedules, provenance,
  9:              chunking, recall, cloze. No I/O, no framework, no logging.
 10: usecases/    Application logic. Interactors, pipeline commands, workflows, and the
 11:              ports (interfaces) the outer layers implement.
 12: adapters/    The controller, the session store, the presenter, in-memory repositories.
 13: frameworks/  React Native UI, AsyncStorage, OpenRouter, DuckDuckGo, fonts, composition.
 14: ```
 15: 
 16: ## Where things live, and why
 17: 
 18: ### `entities/` — what the app is about
 19: 
 20: Pure data and the rules that operate on it. A function belongs here when its answer
 21: depends only on its arguments: `chunkCard`, `makeCloze`, `recallCard`, `gradeSchedule`,
 22: `createOperationRecord`, `normalizeSearchResults`, and the card-tree helpers in
 23: `tree.ts`.
 24: 
 25: Entities perform no I/O and do not log. They used to print every card they created, which
 26: serialized the user's own notes to the console; the dependency test now fails the build if
 27: that comes back.
 28: 
 29: ### `usecases/` — what the app does
 30: 
 31: Three kinds of module live here:
 32: 
 33: **Interactors** — one operation each, depending only on ports:
 34: `RunResearchInteractor`, `GradeReviewInteractor`, `UndoOperationInteractor`,
 35: `GapReportInteractor`.
 36: 
 37: **Pipeline commands** — one stage each, behind the `PipelineCommand` interface. The
 38: `CommandRegistry` knows which exist and builds a `PipelineRunner` from them, so adding a
 39: command touches one file that is not the controller.
 40: 
 41: **Workflows** — a cohesive feature that owns its own state and orchestrates several
 42: interactors. Each exposes `state`, a set of methods, and a `Host` interface describing
 43: what it needs from the app around it:
 44: 
 45: | Workflow | Owns |
 46: |---|---|
 47: | `research/ResearchWorkflow` | search → keep/reject → extract → cited brief |
 48: | `mission/MissionWorkflow` | mission draft, deterministic gap report, syllabus |
 49: | `operations/OperationsWorkflow` | in-flight activity, receipts, undo |
 50: | `review/ReviewSession` | the study queue, reveal state, grading |
 51: 
 52: The `Host` is the seam. A workflow never receives the controller; it receives a small
 53: object saying how to read context (`activeWorkspaceId()`, `apiKey()`) and how to cause
 54: effects (`notify()`, `refreshCards()`, `onChange()`). That is what makes each one testable
 55: with a recording fake and no application around it.
 56: 
 57: **Ports** live in `usecases/ports/`, not in `adapters/`. Ports are defined by the layer
 58: that *needs* them, and implemented further out — that is what keeps the arrows pointing
 59: inward.
 60: 
 61: ### `adapters/` — translating between the app and its delivery
 62: 
 63: - **`GriotController`** — the single object the UI talks to. Its methods are thin;
 64:   the work belongs to workflows and interactors.
 65: - **`AppSessionStore`** — holds `domain` (persisted or derived) and `ui` (on-screen)
 66:   state, owns subscription and the toast timer, and provides the shared edits
 67:   (`select`, `forgetCards`) that more than one feature needs.
 68: - **`presentAppState`** — a pure function projecting domain + ui + every workflow's state
 69:   into the `AppState` the UI renders. Every collection is copied on the way out, so a
 70:   component cannot mutate the session through what it rendered.
 71: - **`repositories/Memory*`** — in-memory implementations used by tests and as safe
 72:   defaults.
 73: 
 74: ### `frameworks/` — the outside world
 75: 
 76: AsyncStorage repositories, the OpenRouter/DuckDuckGo/extraction gateways, fonts, the React
 77: Native UI, and `composition/composeController.ts` — the one module that decides which
 78: concrete implementation stands behind each port.
 79: 
 80: ## Rules the code keeps
 81: 
 82: **Storage failure is never disguised as emptiness.** `JsonCollectionStore` /
 83: `JsonDocumentStore` return an empty collection or `null` *only* when nothing was ever
 84: stored; a failed or corrupt read throws a `PersistenceError`. Read-modify-write is
 85: serialized per store and writes only after a successful read, so a transient read error
 86: cannot truncate the user's cards. A failure at startup surfaces as `storageError` with a
 87: retry, never as a blank library.
 88: 
 89: **Logging policy lives at the edge.** Inner layers report through the `Logger` port;
 90: `ConsoleLogger` in the composition root decides what is printed, and drops `debug`
 91: outside development.
 92: 
 93: **The UI gets copies, not references.** See `presentAppState`.
 94: 
 95: **Refusal beats a partial result.** Undo checks everything before touching anything and
 96: explains why it declined. A research brief that cites nothing is rejected rather than
 97: saved. A missing API key is reported, not silently replaced by a local fallback.
 98: 
 99: ## Testing
100: 
101: ```bash
102: bun test           # unit + architecture tests
103: npx tsc --noEmit   # type check
104: ```
105: 
106: Tests sit in `__tests__/` beside the code they cover. The layering is what makes them
107: cheap: entities are pure, interactors take fakes, workflows take a recording host, and
108: `presentAppState` is a function from data to data. `dependencyRule.test.ts` covers the
109: structure itself — import direction and the no-`console` rule in inner layers.
110: 
111: ## Adding things
112: 
113: **A pipeline command** — implement `PipelineCommand`, register it in `CommandRegistry`.
114: 
115: **A stored collection** — define the port in `usecases/ports/repositories/`, implement it
116: in `frameworks/storage/` on top of `JsonCollectionStore`, wire it in `composeController`.
117: 
118: **A feature with its own state** — write a workflow in `usecases/<feature>/` with a `Host`
119: interface, build it in a `build…()` method on the controller, and read its state in
120: `presentAppState`.
</file>

<file path="docs/goal-architect-implementation-plan.md">
  1: # Goal Architect — implementation plan
  2: 
  3: A conversational first-run and "create goal" experience that turns an ambition into an
  4: inspectable learning mission. This document is the plan the work follows; it is written
  5: before the code so the boundaries are argued once rather than rediscovered per module.
  6: 
  7: ## The promise this feature must not break
  8: 
  9: GRIOT's central claim is that **you can always tell where something came from.** A
 10: goal-planning agent is the easiest place in the app to break that, because plausible
 11: planning prose is cheap to generate and reads like authority. So the design rule is:
 12: 
 13: > Four kinds of statement, never blended: what **you** said, what the **app** computed,
 14: > what the **agent** guessed, and what the **web** actually returned.
 15: 
 16: That is why the working map is not a bag of strings. Every entry carries an
 17: `InsightOrigin`, and the UI renders each origin differently. A model may *propose* a
 18: prerequisite; it may never quietly promote it to a fact the user appears to have stated.
 19: 
 20: Consequences that fall out of the same rule:
 21: 
 22: - The agent never claims to have searched. Only `SearchGateway` searches, and only after
 23:   the user approves a preflight that shows the exact queries.
 24: - A mission proposal creates **nothing** until accepted. The draft says so explicitly.
 25: - A failed model call produces an actionable failure state and **preserves the answers** —
 26:   losing eight answers to one network blip is the bug that makes people distrust the flow.
 27: - No card is enrolled in spaced repetition automatically. Study is a later, explicit act.
 28: 
 29: ## Flow
 30: 
 31: ```
 32: Stage 1  Intent          one question at a time, each with a rationale
 33: Stage 2  Quiet assessment deterministic working map + optional labelled agent hypotheses
 34: Stage 3  Research scan   OPTIONAL, opt-in, real SearchGateway, preflight shows queries
 35: Stage 4  Mission proposal DRAFT — nothing created yet; edit / ask / research / accept
 36: Stage 5  Mission canvas   back to the one canvas, with the created cards
 37: ```
 38: 
 39: Stages 1, 2, and 4 work with **no API key at all**. That is a hard requirement, not a
 40: degraded mode: the question bank, the gap analysis, and the proposal are all deterministic
 41: functions of the user's answers. A model, when present, adds labelled hypotheses and
 42: better phrasing — it is never load-bearing.
 43: 
 44: ## Layering
 45: 
 46: Existing machinery is reused rather than reinvented. `SemanticRole` already has exactly
 47: the vocabulary this feature needs (`goal`, `task`, `experiment`, `concept`, `question`,
 48: `source`, `deliverable`), `Provenance` already records mode/model/web usage, and
 49: `OperationRecord` already carries undo snapshots.
 50: 
 51: | Layer | Module | Responsibility |
 52: |---|---|---|
 53: | entities | `goalArchitect.ts` | Answers, origin-tagged working map, question bank, turn validation, proposal value objects. Pure and serializable. |
 54: | usecases | `goal/GoalArchitectWorkflow.ts` | Session orchestration: ask, answer, skip, edit, request an agent turn, build the proposal. |
 55: | usecases | `goal/CreateMissionPlanInteractor.ts` | Accepted proposal → cards + workspace mission + receipt. The only module here that writes. |
 56: | adapters | `presenters/GoalArchitectPresenter.ts` | Projects session state into what the sheet renders. |
 57: | frameworks | `ui/griot/GoalArchitectSheet.tsx` | The sheet. Reads presenter state, calls controller actions, touches no gateway. |
 58: 
 59: `deriveWorkingMap(answers)` lives in **entities**, not usecases: it is a pure function of
 60: the answers with no I/O, and keeping it pure is what makes "works without an API key"
 61: testable in isolation.
 62: 
 63: ## Question selection
 64: 
 65: The bank is a fixed list of high-leverage questions, each with `id`, `prompt`,
 66: `rationale`, and a `needs` predicate. `selectNextQuestion` returns the first question
 67: whose information is still missing — so answering "I want to ship a playable vertical
 68: slice by March on a Steam Deck" satisfies the deliverable *and* constraint questions at
 69: once and neither is asked. The spec's requirement is "ask only what materially reduces
 70: ambiguity", and a predicate over the current map is how that becomes testable rather than
 71: aspirational.
 72: 
 73: Skipped questions are recorded as skipped, not deleted, so they are never re-asked and the
 74: user can go back and fill one in.
 75: 
 76: ## Agent turn contract
 77: 
 78: The model is asked for a `GoalArchitectTurn` (message, optional question, working map,
 79: optional recommended research). Model output is **validated and normalized before it
 80: reaches the UI** — `normalizeGoalArchitectTurn` returns `null` for anything malformed
 81: rather than letting a half-parsed object render. Everything the model contributes is
 82: tagged `origin: "agent"` on the way in, at the boundary, so nothing downstream has to
 83: remember to do it.
 84: 
 85: ## Receipts and undo
 86: 
 87: Mission acceptance writes one `OperationRecord`: the answer ids consumed, the cards
 88: created with as-created snapshots, whether web and/or a model were used, the model id, and
 89: the destination. `canUndoCreate` already refuses when a created card has been edited
 90: since, which is exactly the required behaviour — undo removes only untouched cards it
 91: created, and a partial undo is never reported as success.
 92: 
 93: ## Increments
 94: 
 95: 1. **Plan** (this document).
 96: 2. **Entities** — `goalArchitect.ts` + tests. Question bank, map derivation, turn
 97:    normalization, proposal building.
 98: 3. **Workflow** — `GoalArchitectWorkflow` + tests. Answer/skip/edit, no-key path, agent
 99:    turn kept separate from app findings, research approval gate.
100: 4. **Mission creation** — `CreateMissionPlanInteractor` + tests. Cards, roles, provenance,
101:    receipt, undo, no auto-scheduling.
102: 5. **Presenter + sheet + entry points** — first run, command palette `goal`, capture menu.
103: 6. **Mission Console appearance profile** — theme mode, accent, surface tint, density.
104:    Deliberately last: it is the only part that changes existing surfaces, and the semantic
105:    colours (warning, error, evidence, selection, success) must stay distinguishable under
106:    every setting. Tracked separately so a theme regression can't hold up the flow.
107: 
108: ## Compatibility
109: 
110: Additive only. `Workspace.mission` already exists and is optional; cards gain no required
111: field. A workspace, card, or settings blob written before this feature loads unchanged,
112: and a user who never opens Goal Architect sees no behavioural difference.
</file>

<file path="docs/ui-system-implementation-plan.md">
  1: # UI System Implementation Plan — Field Shell
  2: 
  3: Phase 0 of the `work.txt` brief. Written before broad implementation, as that brief
  4: requires. It maps every requested surface to what already exists, names what's actually
  5: new, and sequences the work so each phase ships independently testable and the app is
  6: never left mid-migration.
  7: 
  8: ## Reading of the brief
  9: 
 10: `work.txt` asks for a large system: a renamed shell (FIELD), an Agent Studio, a Command
 11: Workshop, a Prompt Architect, an Appearance Studio, and a reworked Field canvas —
 12: around 8 phases of work. Most of the *behavior* it asks for already exists in this
 13: codebase under different names (preflights, receipts, provenance, mission module, gap
 14: report, research bench, review). The work is real, but it is substantially an
 15: **extension and surfacing exercise**, not a rewrite: `work.txt` itself says not to build
 16: a second competing agent/command/theme/storage system, and the inventory below shows
 17: almost everything it names already has a load-bearing counterpart.
 18: 
 19: Two naming conflicts to resolve up front, both cosmetic:
 20: - `work.txt` calls the primary canvas **FIELD**; the existing screen is `SpaceScreen`
 21:   presenting `MissionCanvasHeader` + `ContextCardRow`s. No rename of files is required —
 22:   "FIELD" becomes the header eyebrow/system label; the underlying screen is unchanged.
 23: - `work.txt`'s `AgentProfile` is this codebase's `AssistantProfile` with more policy
 24:   fields. Extended, not replaced (§ Agent Studio below).
 25: 
 26: ## Inventory: what already satisfies the brief
 27: 
 28: | `work.txt` asks for | Already exists as |
 29: |---|---|
 30: | Cards / stdin / stdout / pipelines / receipts | `Card`, `PipelineRunner`, `OperationRecord`, `OperationsWorkflow` |
 31: | Preflight sheet with exact inputs/scope/model/web state | `AgentPreflightPresenter` + `AiPreflightSheet.tsx` |
 32: | Provenance detail | `Provenance` entity, rendered per-card |
 33: | Undo with edited-output refusal | `UndoOperationInteractor`, `canUndoCreate` |
 34: | Mission module with counters + one next action | `MissionCanvasPresenter` + `MissionCanvasHeader` (built this session) |
 35: | Status gap report (deterministic-first) | `GapReportInteractor` + `GapReportSheet.tsx` |
 36: | Research bench / evidence cards / keep-reject-extract | `ResearchWorkflow` + `ResearchResultsSheet.tsx` |
 37: | Cited brief gated on retained evidence | `CreateResearchBriefInteractor` |
 38: | Goal/mission planning (working map, labelled hypotheses, nothing created until confirm) | Folded into the Workspace Agent as the `create_mission` tool intent — see "Goal Architect fold-in" below |
 39: | First-run capture-first entry | `GriotController.init()` first-launch check, which opens the Workspace Agent |
 40: | Selection dock stating count/expansion, routing to preflight | `SelectionTray.tsx` + `selectionActions.ts` |
 41: | Idle dock: Capture/Library/Review/Tools | `BottomNavigation` in `components.tsx` |
 42: | Review loop, predict-then-reveal-capable | `ReviewSession`, `ReviewModal.tsx`, FSRS scheduler |
 43: | Study generation requiring explicit enrollment | `SpaceCommand` (never auto-run) |
 44: | Command palette / typed pipelines / aliases | `CommandRegistry`, `CommandConsoleModal.tsx`, `commandCatalog.ts` |
 45: | Custom commands (macro / agent) | `CommandDefinition` (`agent` \| `pipeline` kinds) |
 46: | Assistant profiles, built-in + user, per-capability | `AssistantProfile`, `resolveAssistantProfile` |
 47: | Appearance: palettes, accent override, fonts, semantic-safe colors | `entities/appearance.ts`, `theme.ts`, `AppearanceSettings.tsx` |
 48: | Font browser: search, preview, install, error states | `SearchFontsInteractor`, `PreviewFontInteractor`, `InstallFontInteractor` (built this session) |
 49: | Reduced motion | `useReducedMotion.ts`, `ArrivalView` |
 50: 
 51: Everything in this table is reused as-is. The plan below only concerns the gaps.
 52: 
 53: ## Real gaps
 54: 
 55: 1. **Agent Studio.** `AssistantProfile` has no `scope` (builtin/global/workspace/session),
 56:    no `contextPolicy`, `webPolicy`, or `outputPolicy`, and there's no list/detail/editor
 57:    UI or duplication flow.
 58: 2. **Prompt Architect.** `OpenRouterAgentGateway.designAssistantProfile` already does the
 59:    model call; there's no preflight/diff/confirm UI around it, and no provenance record
 60:    of an agent-authored prompt revision.
 61: 3. **Command Workshop.** `CommandDefinition` has no scope, input requirements, or output
 62:    role declarations, and there's no stage composer / template list / validation UI.
 63:    Guided-action, research-command, and hybrid-pipeline `kind`s don't exist yet.
 64: 4. **Appearance Studio surface.** The tokens (density, surface tint presets, motion
 65:    level, high contrast) mostly don't exist yet; the current `AppearanceSettings.tsx` is
 66:    palette + font only.
 67: 5. **Tool Deck tile metadata.** `commandCatalog.ts` documents commands textually; it
 68:    doesn't yet drive a tile grid with per-tile disabled-reason.
 69: 6. **FIELD label pass.** Header eyebrows across screens should read `FIELD //…` etc.,
 70:    matching the `work.txt` HUD label list — this is copy-only, using `entities/brand.ts`.
 71: 
 72: ## Phase 1 — Foundations (entities + persistence + tests)
 73: 
 74: ### `entities/assistantProfile.ts` (extend, don't replace)
 75: 
 76: Add, all optional so every persisted profile predating this loads unchanged:
 77: 
 78: ```ts
 79: export type ProfileScope = "builtin" | "global" | "workspace" | "session";
 80: 
 81: export interface ContextPolicy {
 82:   defaultScope: AgentScopeKind; // reuses usecases/agent/AgentScope's existing type
 83:   maxCards: number;
 84:   maxCharacters: number;
 85:   allowSources: boolean;
 86:   allowMission: boolean;
 87:   allowPriorOutputs: boolean;
 88: }
 89: 
 90: export interface OutputPolicy {
 91:   allowCardCreation: boolean;
 92:   allowedRoles: SemanticRole[];
 93:   requireReviewBeforeSave: boolean;
 94:   createGroupForOutputs: boolean;
 95: }
 96: 
 97: // added to AssistantProfile:
 98: scope?: ProfileScope;            // undefined -> treated as "global" (today's behavior)
 99: workspaceId?: string;            // required when scope === "workspace"
100: contextPolicy?: ContextPolicy;   // undefined -> DEFAULT_SCOPE_BUDGET from AgentScope.ts
101: webPolicy?: "never" | "preflight-required"; // undefined -> "never"
102: outputPolicy?: OutputPolicy;
103: isEditable?: boolean;            // undefined -> !builtin
104: sourceProfileId?: string;        // set when duplicated from a builtin
105: ```
106: 
107: `resolveAssistantProfile` gains a `workspaceId` parameter so workspace-scoped profiles
108: are excluded from unrelated workspaces (a `work.txt` persistence requirement); session
109: profiles live only in `AppSessionStore.ui` and are never passed to the repository, so
110: they cannot outlive the process by construction — no separate "clear on restart" logic
111: needed.
112: 
113: ### `entities/commandDefinition.ts` (extend)
114: 
115: ```ts
116: export type CommandScope = "session" | "workspace" | "global";
117: 
118: // added to BaseCommandDefinition:
119: scope?: CommandScope;               // undefined -> "global" (today's behavior)
120: workspaceId?: string;
121: requiredInputRoles?: SemanticRole[];
122: requiredInputCount?: "none" | "one-or-more";
123: webUse?: boolean;                   // declared, not inferred — validated at save time
124: outputRoles?: SemanticRole[];
125: pinned?: boolean;                   // replaces ad hoc pin tracking if any exists; else new
126: ```
127: 
128: New `kind`s added to the discriminated union: `"guided"` (presetId + defaults),
129: `"research"` (always preflight-required), `"hybrid"` (ordered stage list mixing
130: deterministic/agent/research stages). Each is additive to the union — existing `"agent"`
131: and `"pipeline"` commands and every existing typed alias keep working unchanged, and the
132: dependency-rule/pipeline tests already guard that commands round-trip through storage.
133: 
134: ### `entities/appearanceProfile.ts` (new, thin — extends `appearance.ts`)
135: 
136: Rather than a competing token system, this adds the fields `work.txt` asks for onto the
137: existing `AppearanceSettings`/`ResolvedAppearance` shapes:
138: 
139: ```ts
140: // added to AppearanceSettings (all optional):
141: surfaceTint?: "neutral" | "graphite" | "deep-green" | "blue-black" | "warm-paper";
142: density?: "compact" | "standard" | "spacious";
143: motion?: "system" | "reduced" | "full";
144: highContrast?: boolean;
145: ```
146: 
147: `resolveAppearance` grows matching resolved fields with safe defaults
148: (`density: "standard"`, `motion: "system"`, `highContrast: false`). Surface tint is a
149: *modifier* applied to the active palette's neutrals, not a new palette — it cannot touch
150: `danger`/`warning`/`evidence`, preserving the semantic-safety invariant the palette test
151: suite already enforces (`appearance.test.ts`'s "keeps every semantic state
152: distinguishable" test extends to assert this for every tint × palette combination).
153: 
154: ### Persistence
155: 
156: No new storage engine. `AsyncStorageAssistantProfileRepository` and
157: `AsyncStorageCommandDefinitionRepository` already round-trip arbitrary JSON-serializable
158: fields; the new optional fields ride through unchanged. Workspace-scoped profiles/commands
159: are filtered by `workspaceId` at the presenter/workflow boundary (matching how cards are
160: already filtered by `workspaceId`), not by a new repository shape.
161: 
162: **Migration:** none required to *read* old data — every new field is optional and every
163: consumer treats `undefined` as the pre-existing behavior. A one-time normalization on
164: first load (stamp `scope: "global"` on legacy custom profiles/commands so later filtering
165: logic has a concrete value to compare) is applied in the repository's load path, mirroring
166: the existing pattern in `MemorySettingsRepository`/`AsyncStorageSettingsRepository` for
167: backfilling settings fields — write-on-read, never a blocking migration step.
168: 
169: ### Tests (Phase 1)
170: 
171: - `entities/__tests__/assistantProfile.test.ts`: new fields default correctly; legacy
172:   (field-less) profiles resolve identically to before; `resolveAssistantProfile` excludes
173:   workspace-scoped profiles from a different `workspaceId`.
174: - `entities/__tests__/commandDefinition.test.ts`: new `kind`s validate; legacy `agent`/
175:   `pipeline` definitions still construct and normalize identically.
176: - `entities/__tests__/appearance.test.ts`: extend existing semantic-safety test across
177:   tint × palette; density/motion/highContrast default and round-trip.
178: - Repository tests (`AsyncStorageRepositories.test.ts`, `MemoryRepositories.test.ts`):
179:   round-trip the new optional fields; confirm a pre-migration fixture (fields absent)
180:   still loads.
181: 
182: ## Phase 2 — Shell and Field (mostly copy + composition, low risk)
183: 
184: - Header eyebrows updated to the `work.txt` HUD label set via `entities/brand.ts`'s
185:   `systemLabel()` (already the single place these are generated).
186: - `MissionCanvasPresenter`/`ContextCards` already satisfy the card-grammar and
187:   mission-module requirements; this phase only adds the "incomplete/blocked" status
188:   affordance (reads `card.provenance` + open-question role, already available data — no
189:   new entity field).
190: - No new screens. `SpaceScreen` *is* FIELD.
191: 
192: Tests: extend `MissionCanvasPresenter.test.ts` for the new status affordance; snapshot
193: the header label set.
194: 
195: ## Phase 3 — Agent Studio
196: 
197: New modules:
198: - `usecases/agent/AgentProfileWorkflow.ts` — list-by-scope, duplicate-builtin (writes a
199:   new profile with `sourceProfileId` set, `builtin: false`, `isEditable: true`; a builtin
200:   is never mutated in place — enforced by the interactor refusing writes where
201:   `builtin === true`), delete-with-dependents-check (refuses if a `CommandDefinition`
202:   references the profile, per `work.txt`'s deletion-safety requirement).
203: - `adapters/presenters/AgentStudioPresenter.ts` — projects profiles grouped by scope,
204:   each with the plain-language policy summary `work.txt` requires (derived from
205:   `ContextPolicy`/`OutputPolicy`/`webPolicy` — pure string formatting, unit-testable
206:   without a model).
207: - `frameworks/ui/griot/AgentStudioSheet.tsx`, `AgentEditorSheet.tsx` — list + editor,
208:   wired the same way `MissionEditorSheet.tsx` already is: UI reads presenter state,
209:   calls controller actions, never touches `AgentGateway` directly.
210: 
211: Prompt Architect (`usecases/agent/PromptArchitectInteractor.ts`) wraps the existing
212: `AgentGateway.designAssistantProfile` call behind the same preflight-then-diff-then-save
213: shape `GoalArchitectWorkflow` already established: nothing is written until the user
214: confirms, and the confirmed write's `Provenance` records `mode: "agent"` plus the model
215: id, satisfying `work.txt`'s "store provenance that an agent designed/revised the
216: profile" requirement using the existing `Provenance` shape — no new provenance variant.
217: 
218: Tests: profile duplication never mutates the builtin fixture; session-scoped profiles
219: are absent after a simulated restart (fresh `AppSessionStore`); workspace isolation;
220: Prompt Architect requires confirmation before persisting and performs no write on
221: preflight-cancel; malformed model output (reusing the `normalizeGoalArchitectTurn`-style
222: validate-at-the-boundary pattern) is rejected without corrupting the prior profile.
223: 
224: ## Phase 4 — Command Workshop
225: 
226: New modules:
227: - Extends `usecases/commands/CreateCommandDefinitionInteractor.ts` (or a sibling
228:   `CustomCommandWorkflow.ts` if the validation surface grows past what a single
229:   interactor should own — decided during implementation, not the plan, per `work.txt`'s
230:   own "extension of existing command workflow" preference) with the new-`kind` validation
231:   rules: empty-command rejection, unknown-agent-profile rejection, web-without-preflight
232:   rejection, output-role-conflict rejection, stage-input/output type compatibility check
233:   (best-effort — the pipeline is otherwise untyped, so this is advisory, not exhaustive).
234: - `adapters/presenters/CommandWorkshopPresenter.ts` + `frameworks/ui/griot/
235:   CommandWorkshopSheet.tsx` — template list, stage composer, live preflight preview built
236:   from the same `AgentPreflightPresenter` machinery a hybrid command's agent/research
237:   stage would eventually run through.
238: 
239: Tests: every validation rule above as a rejected-input case; existing typed aliases and
240: built-in commands remain executable and untouched by the new validation path (regression
241: guard already exists in `commandCatalog.test.ts`, extended rather than replaced).
242: 
243: ## Phase 5 — Research/evidence and preflight refinement
244: 
245: Mostly presenter-level additions to already-real data:
246: - Source-kind advisory labels (`entities/searchResult.ts` gains a pure classifier
247:   function — official docs / academic / tutorial / etc. — driven by domain heuristics,
248:   never a model call, matching the "no opaque credibility score" requirement).
249: - Provenance detail view surfaces every field `work.txt` lists; all already present on
250:   `Provenance` — this is a UI addition, `ProvenanceDetailSheet.tsx`, not a data change.
251: 
252: Tests: classifier is pure and unit-tested directly; provenance detail presenter test
253: asserts every listed field renders when present and is omitted (not shown as blank) when
254: absent.
255: 
256: ## Phase 6 — Appearance Studio
257: 
258: UI-only, consuming the Phase 1 entity additions: `AppearanceStudioPresenter.ts` +
259: `AppearanceStudioSheet.tsx` (or an extension of `AppearanceSettings.tsx` — likely the
260: latter, since a second sheet competing with the existing one would violate `work.txt`'s
261: own "don't build a competing system" instruction). Surface tint swatches, density
262: toggle, motion toggle, high-contrast toggle, reset-to-defaults action.
263: 
264: Tests: extend `AppearanceSettings`-adjacent presenter tests for every new token's
265: default and round-trip; confirm reduced-motion and high-contrast each produce
266: deterministic, asserted style output (not just "doesn't crash").
267: 
268: ## Phase 7 — Mastery surfaces
269: 
270: Largely already compliant (see inventory table). This phase is a targeted review pass:
271: confirm the review screen states scheduling is separate from card creation (copy check),
272: confirm study-generation drafts are never auto-enrolled (already true —
273: `SpaceCommand` requires an explicit run), add the "open originating card after review"
274: affordance if not already present (`ReviewModal.tsx` — verify during implementation).
275: 
276: ## Phase 8 — Quality pass
277: 
278: Accessibility labeling audit, Android/iOS keyboard-avoidance check on every sheet with a
279: `TextInput` (pattern already established in `GoalArchitectSheet.tsx`/`CaptureScreen.tsx`
280: via `KeyboardAvoidingView`), empty-state audit, and a final full-suite run:
281: `bun test`, `tsc --noEmit`, and the dependency-rule test.
282: 
283: ## Compatibility summary
284: 
285: Every change in every phase is additive: new optional fields, new discriminated-union
286: variants, new files. No existing field is removed or repurposed, no existing repository
287: key changes shape, and every "old data must still load" case has a named test above. A
288: user who never opens Agent Studio, Command Workshop, or Appearance Studio sees no
289: behavioral change at all — the same guarantee this repo's `goal-architect-
290: implementation-plan.md` made for the Goal Architect increment.
291: 
292: ## Sequencing note
293: 
294: This plan is large enough that each phase should land as its own reviewable increment,
295: the same pattern the Goal Architect work used (entities → workflow → presenter/UI →
296: entry points, each its own commit with tests passing throughout). Phase 1 is the
297: dependency for everything after it and is where implementation starts next.
298: 
299: 
300: ## Goal Architect fold-in
301: 
302: The Goal Architect was a second conversational agent with its own sheet, its own staged
303: question flow, and its own model-turn contract. Having two agents meant two places to
304: teach, two prompt bodies to keep honest, and a planning conversation the user could not
305: start from the notes they were already looking at. It is gone; the one Workspace Agent
306: ("Ask GRIOT") now plans missions as an ordinary proposal mid-conversation.
307: 
308: **Deleted**
309: 
310: - `entities/goalArchitect.ts` — the staged question flow (`GOAL_QUESTIONS`,
311:   `selectNextQuestion`, `recordAnswer`, `deriveWorkingMap`, `normalizeGoalArchitectTurn`).
312: - `usecases/goal/GoalArchitectWorkflow.ts` and the whole `usecases/goal/` directory.
313: - `usecases/pipeline/GoalCommand.ts`, its `CommandRegistry` entry, the `goal` entry in
314:   `commandCatalog`, and the `{ kind: "goal" }` pipeline outcome it halted with.
315: - `adapters/presenters/GoalArchitectPresenter.ts` and `frameworks/ui/griot/GoalArchitectSheet.tsx`.
316: - `AgentGateway.designGoalArchitectTurn` + `GoalArchitectTurnResult`, and the
317:   `GoalArchitectCitation` alias, plus the `OpenRouterAgentGateway` implementation.
318: - The `goal-architect` `AgentPromptId`, its prompt definition, the `goal-architect`
319:   `AssistantCapability`, and the `builtin-goal-architect` profile. Profiles already saved
320:   against that capability still load — nothing validates the field on read — they simply
321:   no longer resolve for any capability.
322: - The `{ kind: "goal" }` `SuggestedActionDispatch` variant and its handlers.
323: 
324: **Rehomed, not deleted**
325: 
326: - `entities/mission.ts` — `InsightOrigin`, `Insight`, `WorkingMap`, `EMPTY_WORKING_MAP`,
327:   `RecommendedResearch`, `ProposedCard`, `MissionProposal`, `mergeIntoWorkingMap`,
328:   `buildMissionProposal`, `describeKnownGaps`, `describeAssumptions`. This is exactly the
329:   part that describes a *mission* rather than a *wizard*, which is why it survived.
330: - `usecases/mission/CreateMissionPlanInteractor.ts` — still the one place a proposal
331:   becomes real cards, a workspace mission, and an undoable receipt.
332: 
333: **Where the capability lives now**
334: 
335: `AgentToolIntent` gained `create_mission` (title, goal statement, optional deliverable and
336: success criteria, ordered steps, and the card ids the plan was drawn from).
337: `normalizeWorkspaceAgentResponse` validates it with the same closed-shape, no-salvage rule
338: as every other intent, and rejects card ids the workspace does not contain.
339: `toolIntentItems`/`narrowToolIntent` expose each step and each source card as its own
340: checkbox, so an unwanted step is never created and the completion message counts what
341: actually ran. `GriotController.dispatchWorkspaceAgentTool` dispatches it through
342: `CreateMissionPlanInteractor` with the same `finishWorkspaceAgentDispatch` treatment the
343: other intents get.
344: 
345: The invariants the deleted feature was built around are unchanged: agent-proposed content
346: is tagged `InsightOrigin: "agent"` and carries agent `Provenance`, nothing is created until
347: the user confirms, a mission is never enrolled into review, and the agent still cannot
348: search — it can only propose queries the research preflight runs with approval.
349: 
350: The first-run empty-launch check now opens the Workspace Agent instead of the Goal
351: Architect sheet; `markFirstRun` lived on the deleted workflow and went with it, so the
352: sheet's dismiss control is the agent's ordinary close.
</file>

