<file path="README.md">
  1: # GRIOT
  2: 
  3: GRIOT turns a goal into an evidence-backed capstone. You capture small units of
  4: knowledge, investigate them, practise them, and build from them — and at every step the
  5: app tells you exactly what it is about to do, what it read, and where the result went.
  6: 
  7: It's a mobile learning console built on the Unix philosophy: **cards are the universal
  8: stream, selection is stdin, new results are stdout, and commands compose.** The agent is
  9: one visible, inspectable stage in that pipeline — never an invisible force.
 10: 
 11: ## The promise this app makes
 12: 
 13: Most AI tools leave you guessing whether an answer came from your notes, from the web, or
 14: from the model's imagination. GRIOT's core commitment is that you can always tell:
 15: 
 16: - **Every AI action states its scope before it runs.** A preflight sheet names what will
 17:   be read, whether the web is enabled, what will be created, and where it will land.
 18: - **`Ask` and `Research web` are different operations, and the UI says so.** `ask` calls a
 19:   model but never browses. `search` browses but never calls a model. The command catalog
 20:   records both facts separately (`usesModel`, `usesWeb`) and the interface badges them.
 21: - **Work in flight is always visible.** One activity banner lives in the app shell, so a
 22:   run started on any screen stays visible when you navigate away — labelled `MODEL`,
 23:   `WEB`, or neither.
 24: - **Failures become cards, not disappearing banners.** A failed run lands where its output
 25:   would have gone, survives restart, and carries everything needed to run it again with
 26:   the *original* inputs.
 27: - **Nothing fabricates success.** A missing API key is reported, not silently replaced. A
 28:   research brief that cites nothing is rejected rather than saved. Heuristics are labelled
 29:   as heuristics.
 30: 
 31: ## Core flows
 32: 
 33: **Capture → next actions.** Add a note, source, or question and you get a receipt naming
 34: where it landed plus two or three context-sensitive follow-ups, so capture leads somewhere
 35: instead of dead-ending in a toast.
 36: 
 37: **Mission and gap report.** Give a workspace a goal, success criteria, and a target
 38: deliverable. Mission Control summarises progress; the Status report answers "what do I
 39: have, what's missing, what next?" — computed entirely from your card graph, with **no API
 40: key required**. AI enrichment is a separate, clearly-labelled step on top.
 41: 
 42: **Research.** A real web search via the search gateway, returning inspectable source
 43: candidates with evidence-kind classification and plain-language cautions. Keep, reject,
 44: extract, or open each one. "Save as source" persists a card immediately with no model
 45: involved; "Create cited brief" is the only step that calls one, and it only ever sees the
 46: sources you kept.
 47: 
 48: **Study.** Chunk, recall, cloze, and elaborate turn material into practice; `space` enrols
 49: it into FSRS-5 spaced repetition; `review` runs the due queue. Semantic roles never affect
 50: what's studiable — only a card type's learning behaviour does.
 51: 
 52: **Undo.** The last run can be reversed. If a created card has been edited since, undo
 53: refuses *and explains why* rather than discarding your work — it never applies partially.
 54: 
 55: ## The command line is still there
 56: 
 57: Every capability is reachable by touch *and* as a composable command:
 58: 
 59: ```text
 60: ask "React hooks" | chunk | recall | space
 61: ```
 62: 
 63: The palette documents each command's purpose, what it needs, what it produces, and whether
 64: it touches the web or a model. Canonical actions have `/aliases` (`/research`, `/prereqs`,
 65: `/experiment`, `/study`, `/status`, `/build`). Custom commands and pipeline macros work
 66: alongside the built-ins.
 67: 
 68: ## Make it yours
 69: 
 70: Settings ships DOS-heritage palettes (Amber Phosphor, Green Phosphor, CGA) alongside a
 71: modern dark/light pair, and installs any Google Fonts family by name — no key, no build
 72: step. Palettes apply whole rather than as free-form colour pickers, so no combination can
 73: make a warning read as a heading.
 74: 
 75: Motion is informational: content that just arrived animates in, the selection count pulses
 76: when it changes, and an indeterminate bar shows work in flight (never a fake percentage —
 77: the app cannot know how far a model call is). All of it collapses under the OS
 78: reduce-motion setting, and every animation duplicates something the layout already says in
 79: words.
 80: 
 81: ## Architecture
 82: 
 83: Strict clean architecture, enforced by the dependency rule — inner layers never import
 84: outward:
 85: 
 86: ```
 87: entities/    Cards, workspaces, missions, provenance, schedules, palettes — pure domain
 88: usecases/    Interactors, pipeline commands, feature workflows, and the ports they need
 89: adapters/    Controller, session store, state presenter, in-memory repositories
 90: frameworks/  React Native UI, AsyncStorage, OpenRouter, DuckDuckGo, fonts, composition
 91: ```
 92: 
 93: The UI never imports `usecases/` directly and never calls a gateway; it reads a view-model
 94: and calls controller methods. That view-model is derived on read rather than stored, so a
 95: panel can never describe a card that no longer exists.
 96: 
 97: Each cohesive feature — research, mission, operations/undo, review — is a workflow in
 98: `usecases/` that owns its own state and receives a small `Host` interface for the context
 99: it reads and the effects it causes, so it can be tested with no application around it.
100: 
101: Storage failure is never disguised as emptiness: a failed read throws rather than
102: returning `[]`, so nothing can overwrite your cards with the result of a read that didn't
103: work.
104: 
105: **[docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md)** covers the layers, the rules the code
106: keeps, and where to put new things.
107: 
108: Local-first: everything persists to AsyncStorage on device. No backend, no accounts, no
109: sync. Bring your own OpenRouter key.
110: 
111: ## Getting started
112: 
113: ```bash
114: npm install
115: npm start          # or: npx expo start --tunnel   to test on a device
116: ```
117: 
118: Add an OpenRouter API key in Settings to enable model-backed commands. Everything
119: deterministic — capture, split, recall, cloze, review, grouping, search, the status
120: report — works without one.
121: 
122: ## Tests
123: 
124: ```bash
125: bun test           # 479 tests, including the dependency-rule check
126: npx tsc --noEmit   # type check
127: ```
</file>

<file path="src/adapters/presenters/AgentPreflightPresenter.ts">
 1: import { AppState } from "./GriotController";
 2: import { AgentRunRequest, OperationPreset, buildAgentRunRequest, findOperationPreset } from "../../usecases/agent/operationPresets";
 3: import { expandForPipe } from "../../entities/tree";
 4: export interface AgentPreflightModel {
 5:   preset: OperationPreset;
 6:   request: AgentRunRequest;
 7:   selectedCount: number;
 8:   contextPreview: string[];
 9:   hasApiKey: boolean;
10:   modelLabel: string;
11:   webEnabled: boolean;
12:   runLabel: string;
13:   blockedReason: string | null;
14:   warning: string | null;
15:   querySuggestions: string[];
16: }
17: export function presentAgentPreflight(
18:   state: AppState,
19:   presetId: string,
20:   query: string
21: ): AgentPreflightModel | null {
22:   const preset = findOperationPreset(presetId);
23:   if (!preset) return null;
24:   const selectedCards = expandForPipe(state.cards, state.selection);
25:   const request = buildAgentRunRequest({
26:     preset,
27:     workspaceId: state.activeWorkspaceId ?? "",
28:     parentId: state.currentGroupId,
29:     allCards: state.cards,
30:     selectedCards,
31:     query,
32:   });
33:   const hasApiKey = Boolean(state.openRouterKey?.trim());
34:   const webEnabled = request.webUsed;
35:   const needsAgent = preset.command === "ask";
36:   let blockedReason: string | null = null;
37:   if (preset.requiresSelection && selectedCards.length === 0) {
38:     blockedReason = "Select at least one card first";
39:   } else if (preset.requiresQuery && !query.trim()) {
40:     blockedReason = "Enter a search query";
41:   } else if (!state.activeWorkspaceId) {
42:     blockedReason = "Create a workspace first";
43:   }
44:   const warning =
45:     !blockedReason && needsAgent && !hasApiKey
46:       ? "No OpenRouter API key configured — this will fall back to local template cards, not a real model response."
47:       : null;
48:   const previewCards = request.contextCardIds
49:     .map(id => state.cards.find(c => c.id === id))
50:     .filter((c): c is NonNullable<typeof c> => Boolean(c));
51:   const runLabel = buildRunLabel(preset, selectedCards.length, query);
52:   const querySuggestions = preset.requiresQuery
53:     ? buildQuerySuggestions(state, selectedCards)
54:     : [];
55:   return {
56:     preset,
57:     request,
58:     selectedCount: selectedCards.length,
59:     contextPreview: previewCards.slice(0, 5).map(c => c.title),
60:     hasApiKey,
61:     modelLabel: state.selectedModel?.trim() || "default model",
62:     webEnabled,
63:     runLabel,
64:     blockedReason,
65:     warning,
66:     querySuggestions,
67:   };
68: }
69: function buildQuerySuggestions(
70:   state: AppState,
71:   selectedCards: { title: string }[]
72: ): string[] {
73:   const suggestions: string[] = [];
74:   const seen = new Set<string>();
75:   const push = (text?: string | null) => {
76:     const trimmed = text?.trim();
77:     if (!trimmed) return;
78:     const key = trimmed.toLowerCase();
79:     if (seen.has(key)) return;
80:     seen.add(key);
81:     suggestions.push(trimmed);
82:   };
83:   for (const card of selectedCards.slice(0, 2)) push(card.title);
84:   const uncovered = state.gapReport?.successCriteria.filter(c => !c.hasEvidence) ?? [];
85:   for (const criterion of uncovered.slice(0, 2)) push(criterion.text);
86:   if (state.gapReport?.hasMission) push(state.gapReport.missionTitle);
87:   return suggestions.slice(0, 4);
88: }
89: function buildRunLabel(preset: OperationPreset, selectedCount: number, query: string): string {
90:   if (preset.id === "research-web") {
91:     const q = query.trim();
92:     return q ? `Research "${q}" on the web` : "Research on the web";
93:   }
94:   if (preset.requiresSelection) {
95:     return `${preset.label} (${selectedCount} selected)`;
96:   }
97:   return preset.label;
98: }
</file>

